Harbor: RtlValidRelativeSecurityDescriptor accepts valid descriptors (#91).

The function returned RtlValidSecurityDescriptor(descriptor) == STATUS_SUCCESS. RtlValidSecurityDescriptor returns
a BOOLEAN, so comparing it with 0 turned the answer round: every valid descriptor was reported invalid, and an
invalid one valid.

Microsoft's offreg.dll (the offline registry library; Office's Click-to-Run installer ships its own copy) checks
each hive's security descriptors with it and refuses a hive that fails: OROpenHive returns
ERROR_NOT_REGISTRY_FILE. The installer then wrote its ten root\vreg\*.vreg.dat hives as empty 8 KB files and
reported success, and Office's private registry (HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Office\ClickToRun\REGISTRY) held 24 keys
instead of about 14,000: no VBA, and automation could not return workbook objects.

Still a semi-stub: the length and the requested parts (info) are not checked.

Test: WindowsTests/secdesc/run.sh.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/sec.c	2026-11-26 15:37:13
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/sec.c	2026-11-26 15:37:13
@@ -551,7 +551,7 @@
     ULONG length, SECURITY_INFORMATION info)
 {
     FIXME("%p,%lu,%ld: semi-stub\n", descriptor, length, info);
-    return RtlValidSecurityDescriptor(descriptor) == STATUS_SUCCESS;
+    return RtlValidSecurityDescriptor(descriptor);
 }
 
